<section class="paper">
  <div class="wrap story">
    <div class="side rv">
      <span class="k mono">\ The problem</span>
      <h2>Stop wagging the <span class="serif">finger.</span></h2>
      <p class="stat46"><b>46%</b><span>of Idaho grads went on after high school. One of the lowest rates in the country.</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="body rv">
      <p>A statewide campaign to lift Idaho's college-going rate by talking with teens instead of at them. I shot the campaign and worked the experiential build that turned a hard conversation about the future into one that happened 18,000+ times between 2015 and 2018.</p>
      <p>The usual fix is an adult telling a teen what they should do. The campaign had a name for those people: They Sayers. Nobody listens to a They Sayer.</p>
      <p>So Buck the Quo did the opposite. It talked to the teens themselves, not their parents, not their teachers, and treated them like the people actually making the decision. The brand had to feel like theirs before it could change a single mind.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="film">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="bar">
      <span class="mono"><b>The films</b> · Buck the Quo</span>
      <span class="mono" style="color:#8f8d88">Play them here</span>
    </div>
    <div class="poster16" data-src="/case-studies/buck-the-quo/films/film-01.mp4" data-title="Hella Bright Highlights · Buck the Quo">
